[{"location":"decoding.html#Decoding","page":"Decoding","title":"Decoding","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"decoding.html#Overview","page":"Decoding","title":"Overview","text":"Decoding recovers the most likely hidden state sequence from observed data, or computes filtered state probabilities.","category":"section"},{"location":"decoding.html#Viterbi-Decoding","page":"Decoding","title":"Viterbi Decoding","text":"Find the most likely state sequence given observed excess growth rates:\n\nG = excess_growth_rates(prices; rf=0.05)\nstates = decode(model, G)","category":"section"},{"location":"decoding.html#Forward-Filtering","page":"Decoding","title":"Forward Filtering","text":"Compute filtered state probabilities P(S_t = k | G_{1:t}):\n\nalpha = forward_filter(model, G)\n# alpha is (n_steps × N) matrix, each row sums to 1","category":"section"},{"location":"decoding.html#Log-Likelihood","page":"Decoding","title":"Log-Likelihood","text":"Compute the log-likelihood of an observation sequence:\n\nll = log_likelihood(model, G)","category":"section"},{"location":"decoding.html#API","page":"Decoding","title":"API","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"finance.html#Finance-Helpers","page":"Finance Helpers","title":"Finance Helpers","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"finance.html#Overview","page":"Finance Helpers","title":"Overview","text":"Built-in functions for computing excess growth rates and reconstructing price paths.","category":"section"},{"location":"finance.html#Excess-Growth-Rates","page":"Finance Helpers","title":"Excess Growth Rates","text":"Annualized excess log-growth rates:\n\nG_t = frac1Delta t lnleft(fracP_tP_t-1right) - r_f\n\nSupports three input formats:\n\n# From a price vector\nG = excess_growth_rates(prices; rf=0.05, dt=1/252)\n\n# From a price matrix (n_obs × n_assets)\nG = excess_growth_rates(price_matrix; rf=0.05, dt=1/252)\n\n# From a Dict{String, DataFrame}\nG = excess_growth_rates(data, tickers; rf=0.05, price_col=:close)","category":"section"},{"location":"finance.html#Price-Reconstruction","page":"Finance Helpers","title":"Price Reconstruction","text":"Convert excess growth rates back to prices:\n\nP = prices_from_growth_rates(G, P0; rf=0.05, dt=1/252)\n\nThis is the exact inverse: prices_from_growth_rates(excess_growth_rates(p, rf=r), p[1], rf=r) ≈ p.","category":"section"},{"location":"finance.html#API","page":"Finance Helpers","title":"API","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"sim.html#Single-Index-Model","page":"Single-Index Model","title":"Single-Index Model","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"sim.html#Overview","page":"Single-Index Model","title":"Overview","text":"The Single-Index Model (SIM) is an alternative to copulas for modeling cross-asset dependence. All correlation flows through a single market factor:\n\nG_i = alpha_i + beta_i times G_textmarket + varepsilon_i\n\nwhere αᵢ is the intercept, βᵢ is the market beta, and εᵢ ~ N(0, σ_εᵢ) is the idiosyncratic residual.","category":"section"},{"location":"sim.html#When-to-Use","page":"Single-Index Model","title":"When to Use","text":"Large universes (100+ assets): SIM needs only 2 parameters per asset vs n(n-1)/2 for copulas\nPaper reproducibility: The paper uses SIM for the 424 S&P 500 constituent extension\nQuick baseline: Simpler to fit and understand","category":"section"},{"location":"sim.html#Trade-offs-vs-Copulas","page":"Single-Index Model","title":"Trade-offs vs Copulas","text":"Cannot capture sector-level co-movement independent of the market factor\nNo explicit tail dependence control (inherited from market HMM)\nAll pairwise correlations are determined by the betas: cor(i,j) ≈ βᵢβⱼσ²_market","category":"section"},{"location":"sim.html#Usage","page":"Single-Index Model","title":"Usage","text":"sim = fit(SingleIndexModel, asset_returns, market_returns;\n          market_prices=market_prices, N=100, ν=5.0)\n\n# Generate correlated asset returns\ndep_returns = sample_dependence(sim, 252)","category":"section"},{"location":"sim.html#API","page":"Single-Index Model","title":"API","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"copulas.html#Copulas","page":"Copulas","title":"Copulas","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"copulas.html#Overview","page":"Copulas","title":"Overview","text":"Copulas capture cross-asset dependence without modifying the marginal distributions. Each asset gets its own Jump-HMM for marginal dynamics, while a copula models how the assets co-move.\n\nTwo copula types are available:\n\nCopula Tail Dependence Parameters Best For\nGaussianCopula None Correlation matrix Σ Simple baseline\nStudentTCopula Yes (via ν) Correlation matrix Σ + ν Realistic portfolios","category":"section"},{"location":"copulas.html#Gaussian-Copula","page":"Copulas","title":"Gaussian Copula","text":"Captures linear dependence via a correlation matrix. Simple but underestimates the tendency for assets to crash together.\n\ngc = fit(GaussianCopula, returns_matrix)\nU = sample_dependence(gc, 1000)  # (1000 × n_assets) uniform matrix","category":"section"},{"location":"copulas.html#Student-t-Copula","page":"Copulas","title":"Student-t Copula","text":"Adds a degrees-of-freedom parameter ν that controls tail dependence — the tendency for extreme co-movements. Lower ν means stronger tail dependence.\n\ntc = fit(StudentTCopula, returns_matrix)\nU = sample_dependence(tc, 1000)","category":"section"},{"location":"copulas.html#API","page":"Copulas","title":"API","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"portfolio.html#Portfolio","page":"Portfolio","title":"Portfolio","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"portfolio.html#Overview","page":"Portfolio","title":"Overview","text":"The PortfolioModel combines marginal Jump-HMMs with a dependence model to simulate correlated multi-asset portfolios. The interface is identical regardless of which dependence model is used.","category":"section"},{"location":"portfolio.html#Copula-Approach","page":"Portfolio","title":"Copula Approach","text":"tickers = [\"AAPL\", \"MSFT\", \"JPM\", \"XOM\", \"JNJ\"]\nprice_matrix = [...]  # (n_obs × 5)\n\nportfolio = fit(PortfolioModel, tickers, price_matrix;\n    dependence=StudentTCopula, rf=0.05, N=100)\n\nportfolio = tune(portfolio, price_matrix)\nresult = simulate(portfolio, 252; n_paths=1000)\nreports = validate(portfolio, price_matrix)","category":"section"},{"location":"portfolio.html#Single-Index-Model-Approach","page":"Portfolio","title":"Single-Index Model Approach","text":"tickers = [\"SPY\", \"AAPL\", \"MSFT\", ...]  # include market ticker\nprice_matrix = [...]\n\nportfolio = fit(PortfolioModel, tickers, price_matrix;\n    dependence=SingleIndexModel, market=\"SPY\", rf=0.05)\n\nportfolio = tune(portfolio, price_matrix)\nresult = simulate(portfolio, 252; n_paths=1000)","category":"section"},{"location":"portfolio.html#Comparing-Approaches","page":"Portfolio","title":"Comparing Approaches","text":"portfolio_copula = fit(PortfolioModel, tickers, prices;\n    dependence=StudentTCopula, rf=0.05)\nportfolio_sim = fit(PortfolioModel, tickers, prices;\n    dependence=SingleIndexModel, market=\"SPY\", rf=0.05)\n\nreports_copula = validate(portfolio_copula, prices)\nreports_sim = validate(portfolio_sim, prices)","category":"section"},{"location":"portfolio.html#API","page":"Portfolio","title":"API","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"tuning.html#Tuning","page":"Tuning","title":"Tuning","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"tuning.html#Overview","page":"Tuning","title":"Overview","text":"The tune function performs a grid search over jump parameters (ϵ, λ) to find values that best balance distributional fidelity (kurtosis) and temporal fidelity (autocorrelation of absolute returns).\n\nThe objective function is:\n\nJ(epsilon lambda) = sum_ell=1^L (textacf_textobsell - textacf_textsimell)^2 + w_kappa cdot (kappa_textobs - kappa_textsim)^2\n\nOnly simulated paths containing at least one jump event contribute to the objective, matching the paper's methodology.","category":"section"},{"location":"tuning.html#Usage","page":"Tuning","title":"Usage","text":"tuned_model = tune(model, prices;\n    ϵ_range=range(1e-4, 2.5e-2, length=20),\n    λ_range=range(10, 160, length=16),\n    n_paths=200,\n    w_κ=0.20)\n\nThe returned model is a new JumpHiddenMarkovModel with optimized JumpParameters. The partition, transition matrix, and emissions are carried forward unchanged.","category":"section"},{"location":"tuning.html#API","page":"Tuning","title":"API","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"vine.html#Vine-Copula","page":"Vine Copula","title":"Vine Copula","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"vine.html#Overview","page":"Vine Copula","title":"Overview","text":"A vine copula (C-vine) models cross-asset dependence by assigning a separate bivariate copula to each pair of assets. Unlike Gaussian or Student-t copulas that apply a single dependence structure uniformly, vine copulas allow heterogeneous dependence — tech stocks can crash together (Clayton, lower tail) while energy-finance pairs have symmetric dependence (Gaussian).","category":"section"},{"location":"vine.html#Bivariate-Copula-Families","page":"Vine Copula","title":"Bivariate Copula Families","text":"Five families are available, automatically selected per edge via AIC:\n\nFamily Tail Dependence Symmetry Use Case\nGaussianBiCopula None Symmetric Weak/normal dependence\nStudentTBiCopula Both tails Symmetric Joint crashes + rallies\nClaytonBiCopula Lower tail Asymmetric Crash-together, diverge in rallies\nGumbelBiCopula Upper tail Asymmetric Rally-together, diverge in crashes\nFrankBiCopula None Symmetric Moderate, no tail dependence","category":"section"},{"location":"vine.html#How-It-Works","page":"Vine Copula","title":"How It Works","text":"The C-vine organizes variables into a tree structure:\n\nTree 1: The most connected variable (highest sum of |Kendall's tau|) is the center. Bivariate copulas link it to all other variables.\nTree 2: Conditional dependencies are modeled using pseudo-observations computed via h-functions from Tree 1.\nTrees 3..d-1: Higher-order conditional dependencies.\n\nEach edge in each tree gets the bivariate family that minimizes AIC, so the model adapts to the specific dependence pattern of each pair.","category":"section"},{"location":"vine.html#Usage","page":"Vine Copula","title":"Usage","text":"using JumpHMM\n\n# Fit a vine copula from returns\nvc = fit(VineCopula, returns_matrix)\n\n# Sample correlated uniforms\nU = sample_dependence(vc, 1000)\n\n# Use in a portfolio model\nportfolio = fit(PortfolioModel, tickers, price_matrix;\n    dependence=VineCopula, rf=0.05, N=100)","category":"section"},{"location":"vine.html#When-to-Use","page":"Vine Copula","title":"When to Use","text":"20-100 assets: Where heterogeneous dependence matters but copula estimation is still feasible\nSector-diverse portfolios: Where different sectors have different dependence patterns\nTail-risk analysis: Where asymmetric tail dependence (crashes vs rallies) is important","category":"section"},{"location":"api.html#API-Reference","page":"API Reference","title":"API Reference","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"api.html#Types","page":"API Reference","title":"Types","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"api.html#Abstract-Types","page":"API Reference","title":"Abstract Types","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"api.html#Model-Types","page":"API Reference","title":"Model Types","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"api.html#Simulation-Types","page":"API Reference","title":"Simulation Types","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"api.html#Validation-Types","page":"API Reference","title":"Validation Types","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"api.html#Dependence-Models","page":"API Reference","title":"Dependence Models","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"api.html#Bivariate-Copula-Families","page":"API Reference","title":"Bivariate Copula Families","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"api.html#Portfolio-Types","page":"API Reference","title":"Portfolio Types","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"api.html#Functions","page":"API Reference","title":"Functions","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"api.html#Fitting","page":"API Reference","title":"Fitting","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"api.html#Tuning","page":"API Reference","title":"Tuning","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"api.html#Simulation","page":"API Reference","title":"Simulation","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"api.html#Decoding","page":"API Reference","title":"Decoding","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"api.html#Validation","page":"API Reference","title":"Validation","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"api.html#Dependence","page":"API Reference","title":"Dependence","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"api.html#Finance-Helpers","page":"API Reference","title":"Finance Helpers","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"api.html#JumpHMM.AbstractMarkovModel","page":"API Reference","title":"JumpHMM.AbstractMarkovModel","text":"Supertype for all Markov models in the package.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api.html#JumpHMM.AbstractDependenceModel","page":"API Reference","title":"JumpHMM.AbstractDependenceModel","text":"Supertype for copula-based and factor-based dependence models.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api.html#JumpHMM.AbstractValidationResult","page":"API Reference","title":"JumpHMM.AbstractValidationResult","text":"Supertype for validation report types.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api.html#JumpHMM.LaplacePartition","page":"API Reference","title":"JumpHMM.LaplacePartition","text":"LaplacePartition\n\nDiscretization of continuous observations into N states using equal-probability quantile bins of a fitted Laplace distribution.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api.html#JumpHMM.StudentTEmission","page":"API Reference","title":"JumpHMM.StudentTEmission","text":"StudentTEmission\n\nPer-state emission: location-scale Student-t.     Gt | St = k  ~  μk + σk × t_ν\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api.html#JumpHMM.JumpParameters","page":"API Reference","title":"JumpHMM.JumpParameters","text":"JumpParameters\n\nPoisson-driven jump mechanism that forces the chain into tail states.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api.html#JumpHMM.JumpHiddenMarkovModel","page":"API Reference","title":"JumpHMM.JumpHiddenMarkovModel","text":"JumpHiddenMarkovModel <: AbstractMarkovModel\n\nFitted single-asset Jump-HMM. Immutable — fitting/tuning return new instances.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api.html#JumpHMM.SimulationPath","page":"API Reference","title":"JumpHMM.SimulationPath","text":"SimulationPath\n\nA single simulated path: states, observations, and jump indicators.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api.html#JumpHMM.SimulationResult","page":"API Reference","title":"JumpHMM.SimulationResult","text":"SimulationResult\n\nCollection of simulated paths.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api.html#JumpHMM.PathTestResult","page":"API Reference","title":"JumpHMM.PathTestResult","text":"PathTestResult\n\nStatistical test results for a single simulated path vs empirical data.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api.html#JumpHMM.ValidationReport","page":"API Reference","title":"JumpHMM.ValidationReport","text":"ValidationReport <: AbstractValidationResult\n\nAggregate validation report across Monte Carlo paths.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api.html#JumpHMM.GaussianCopula","page":"API Reference","title":"JumpHMM.GaussianCopula","text":"GaussianCopula <: AbstractDependenceModel\n\nGaussian copula defined by a correlation matrix.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api.html#JumpHMM.StudentTCopula","page":"API Reference","title":"JumpHMM.StudentTCopula","text":"StudentTCopula <: AbstractDependenceModel\n\nStudent-t copula with tail dependence.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api.html#JumpHMM.VineCopula","page":"API Reference","title":"JumpHMM.VineCopula","text":"VineCopula <: AbstractDependenceModel\n\nC-vine copula: each pair of assets gets its own bivariate copula family, allowing heterogeneous dependence (e.g., heavy-tailed for tech pairs, asymmetric for energy-finance). Automatic family selection via AIC.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api.html#JumpHMM.SingleIndexModel","page":"API Reference","title":"JumpHMM.SingleIndexModel","text":"SingleIndexModel <: AbstractDependenceModel\n\nSingle-Index Model: all cross-asset correlation flows through one market factor.     Gi = αᵢ + βᵢ × Gmarket + εᵢ\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api.html#JumpHMM.AbstractBivariateCopula","page":"API Reference","title":"JumpHMM.AbstractBivariateCopula","text":"Supertype for bivariate copula families used in vine copula construction.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api.html#JumpHMM.GaussianBiCopula","page":"API Reference","title":"JumpHMM.GaussianBiCopula","text":"Gaussian bivariate copula with correlation parameter ρ ∈ (-1, 1).\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api.html#JumpHMM.StudentTBiCopula","page":"API Reference","title":"JumpHMM.StudentTBiCopula","text":"Student-t bivariate copula with correlation ρ and degrees of freedom ν.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api.html#JumpHMM.ClaytonBiCopula","page":"API Reference","title":"JumpHMM.ClaytonBiCopula","text":"Clayton bivariate copula with parameter θ > 0. Lower tail dependence.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api.html#JumpHMM.GumbelBiCopula","page":"API Reference","title":"JumpHMM.GumbelBiCopula","text":"Gumbel bivariate copula with parameter θ ≥ 1. Upper tail dependence.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api.html#JumpHMM.FrankBiCopula","page":"API Reference","title":"JumpHMM.FrankBiCopula","text":"Frank bivariate copula with parameter θ ≠ 0. No tail dependence, symmetric.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api.html#JumpHMM.VineEdge","page":"API Reference","title":"JumpHMM.VineEdge","text":"VineEdge\n\nOne edge in a vine copula tree, representing a conditional bivariate copula.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api.html#JumpHMM.PortfolioModel","page":"API Reference","title":"JumpHMM.PortfolioModel","text":"PortfolioModel\n\nMulti-asset model: marginal Jump-HMMs coupled by a dependence model.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api.html#JumpHMM.PortfolioSimulationResult","page":"API Reference","title":"JumpHMM.PortfolioSimulationResult","text":"PortfolioSimulationResult\n\nCorrelated synthetic paths across multiple assets.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api.html#JumpHMM.fit-Tuple{Type{JumpHiddenMarkovModel}, AbstractVector{<:Real}}","page":"API Reference","title":"JumpHMM.fit","text":"fit(::Type{JumpHiddenMarkovModel}, prices; kwargs...) → JumpHiddenMarkovModel\n\nFit a Jump-HMM from a price time series. Returns a model with no active jump mechanism (ϵ=0). Call tune to optimize jump parameters.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api.html#JumpHMM.fit-Tuple{Type{LaplacePartition}, AbstractVector{Float64}}","page":"API Reference","title":"JumpHMM.fit","text":"fit(::Type{LaplacePartition}, observations; N=100) → LaplacePartition\n\nFit a Laplace distribution via MLE, then construct N equal-probability quantile bins.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api.html#JumpHMM.fit-Tuple{Type{GaussianCopula}, AbstractMatrix{Float64}}","page":"API Reference","title":"JumpHMM.fit","text":"fit(::Type{GaussianCopula}, returns) → GaussianCopula\n\nFit a Gaussian copula from an (nobs × nassets) matrix of excess growth rates.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api.html#JumpHMM.fit-Tuple{Type{StudentTCopula}, AbstractMatrix{Float64}}","page":"API Reference","title":"JumpHMM.fit","text":"fit(::Type{StudentTCopula}, returns) → StudentTCopula\n\nFit a Student-t copula. Estimates correlation from PIT values and ν via profile MLE.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api.html#JumpHMM.fit-Tuple{Type{VineCopula}, AbstractMatrix{Float64}}","page":"API Reference","title":"JumpHMM.fit","text":"fit(::Type{VineCopula}, returns) → VineCopula\n\nFit a C-vine copula from an (nobs × nassets) matrix of excess growth rates. Automatically selects bivariate copula families per edge via AIC. Variable ordering: most connected variable (by sum of |Kendall's tau|) is placed at the center of tree 1.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api.html#JumpHMM.fit-Tuple{Type{SingleIndexModel}, AbstractMatrix{Float64}, AbstractVector{Float64}}","page":"API Reference","title":"JumpHMM.fit","text":"fit(::Type{SingleIndexModel}, returns, market_returns; kwargs...) → SingleIndexModel\n\nFit a Single-Index Model. For each asset, estimates α, β, σε via OLS:     Gi = αᵢ + βᵢ × G_market + εᵢ\n\nAlso fits a JumpHiddenMarkovModel to the market returns.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api.html#JumpHMM.fit-Tuple{Type{PortfolioModel}, Vector{String}, AbstractMatrix{<:Real}}","page":"API Reference","title":"JumpHMM.fit","text":"fit(::Type{PortfolioModel}, tickers, prices; kwargs...) → PortfolioModel\n\nFit a multi-asset model with the specified dependence structure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api.html#JumpHMM.assign_states","page":"API Reference","title":"JumpHMM.assign_states","text":"assign_states(partition, observations) → Vector{Int}\n\nMap continuous observations to discrete state indices.\n\nConvention: boundaries[k] ≤ x < boundaries[k+1] for k = 1..N-1. Last state (k = N) is inclusive on the upper bound.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api.html#JumpHMM.estimate_transition","page":"API Reference","title":"JumpHMM.estimate_transition","text":"estimate_transition(states, N) → Matrix{Float64}\n\nEstimate an N×N row-stochastic transition matrix by frequency counting. Rows with zero counts are filled with uniform 1/N.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api.html#JumpHMM.stationary_distribution","page":"API Reference","title":"JumpHMM.stationary_distribution","text":"stationary_distribution(T; power=50) → Vector{Float64}\n\nCompute the stationary distribution via matrix exponentiation: π̄ = (T^power)[1, :].\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api.html#JumpHMM.fit_emissions","page":"API Reference","title":"JumpHMM.fit_emissions","text":"fit_emissions(states, observations, N; ν=5.0, min_obs=2) → Vector{StudentTEmission}\n\nFit per-state Student-t emission distributions. States with fewer than min_obs observations (or σ < 1e-12) fall back to global mean/std.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api.html#JumpHMM.sample_emission","page":"API Reference","title":"JumpHMM.sample_emission","text":"sample_emission(e::StudentTEmission) → Float64\n\nDraw a single sample: x = μ + σ × rand(TDist(ν)).\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api.html#JumpHMM.tune-Tuple{JumpHiddenMarkovModel, AbstractVector{<:Real}}","page":"API Reference","title":"JumpHMM.tune","text":"tune(model, prices; kwargs...) → JumpHiddenMarkovModel\n\nGrid search over (ϵ, λ) to minimize a composite objective balancing distributional fidelity (kurtosis) and temporal fidelity (ACF of |returns|).\n\nReturns a NEW model with optimized JumpParameters.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api.html#JumpHMM.tune-Tuple{PortfolioModel, AbstractMatrix{<:Real}}","page":"API Reference","title":"JumpHMM.tune","text":"tune(portfolio, prices; kwargs...) → PortfolioModel\n\nTune jump parameters for each marginal model. Returns a new PortfolioModel.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api.html#JumpHMM.simulate-Tuple{JumpHiddenMarkovModel, Int64}","page":"API Reference","title":"JumpHMM.simulate","text":"simulate(model, n_steps; n_paths=1000, start=:stationary, seed=nothing) → SimulationResult\n\nGenerate synthetic paths from a fitted Jump-HMM.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api.html#JumpHMM.simulate-Tuple{PortfolioModel, Int64}","page":"API Reference","title":"JumpHMM.simulate","text":"simulate(portfolio, n_steps; kwargs...) → PortfolioSimulationResult\n\nGenerate correlated synthetic paths across all assets.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api.html#JumpHMM.decode","page":"API Reference","title":"JumpHMM.decode","text":"decode(model, observations) → Vector{Int}\n\nViterbi algorithm: find the most likely state sequence. Computed in log-space.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api.html#JumpHMM.forward_filter","page":"API Reference","title":"JumpHMM.forward_filter","text":"forward_filter(model, observations) → Matrix{Float64}\n\nForward algorithm: compute P(St = k | G{1:t}). Returns (n_steps × N) matrix.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api.html#JumpHMM.log_likelihood","page":"API Reference","title":"JumpHMM.log_likelihood","text":"log_likelihood(model, observations) → Float64\n\nLog-likelihood of an observation sequence under the model (forward algorithm).\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api.html#JumpHMM.validate-Tuple{JumpHiddenMarkovModel, AbstractVector{<:Real}}","page":"API Reference","title":"JumpHMM.validate","text":"validate(model, prices; kwargs...) → ValidationReport\n\nStatistical validation: simulate paths and compare against empirical data.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api.html#JumpHMM.validate-Tuple{PortfolioModel, AbstractMatrix{<:Real}}","page":"API Reference","title":"JumpHMM.validate","text":"validate(portfolio, prices; kwargs...) → Dict{String, ValidationReport}\n\nValidate each marginal model against its empirical data.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api.html#JumpHMM.sample_dependence-Tuple{GaussianCopula, Int64}","page":"API Reference","title":"JumpHMM.sample_dependence","text":"sample_dependence(gc::GaussianCopula, n) → Matrix{Float64}\n\nDraw n samples from the Gaussian copula. Returns (n × d) uniform matrix.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api.html#JumpHMM.sample_dependence-Tuple{StudentTCopula, Int64}","page":"API Reference","title":"JumpHMM.sample_dependence","text":"sample_dependence(tc::StudentTCopula, n) → Matrix{Float64}\n\nDraw n samples from the Student-t copula. Returns (n × d) uniform matrix.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api.html#JumpHMM.sample_dependence-Tuple{VineCopula, Int64}","page":"API Reference","title":"JumpHMM.sample_dependence","text":"sample_dependence(vc::VineCopula, n) → Matrix{Float64}\n\nDraw n samples from the C-vine copula. Returns (n × d) uniform matrix.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api.html#JumpHMM.sample_dependence-Tuple{SingleIndexModel, Int64}","page":"API Reference","title":"JumpHMM.sample_dependence","text":"sample_dependence(sim::SingleIndexModel, n) → Matrix{Float64}\n\nSimulate market returns via HMM, then generate asset returns:     Gi = αᵢ + βᵢ × Gmarket + εᵢ\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api.html#JumpHMM.excess_growth_rates-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:Real}}","page":"API Reference","title":"JumpHMM.excess_growth_rates","text":"excess_growth_rates(prices; rf=0.0, dt=1/252) → Vector{Float64}\n\nCompute annualized excess log-growth rates:     Gt = (1/dt) × ln(Pt / P_{t-1}) - rf\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api.html#JumpHMM.excess_growth_rates-Tuple{AbstractMatrix{<:Real}}","page":"API Reference","title":"JumpHMM.excess_growth_rates","text":"excess_growth_rates(prices; rf=0.0, dt=1/252) → Matrix{Float64}\n\nColumn-wise excess growth rates from an (nobs × nassets) price matrix.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api.html#JumpHMM.excess_growth_rates-Tuple{Dict{String, DataFrame}, Vector{String}}","page":"API Reference","title":"JumpHMM.excess_growth_rates","text":"excess_growth_rates(data, tickers; rf=0.0, dt=1/252, price_col=:close) → Matrix{Float64}\n\nCompute excess growth rates from a Dict{String, DataFrame} of price data.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api.html#JumpHMM.prices_from_growth_rates","page":"API Reference","title":"JumpHMM.prices_from_growth_rates","text":"prices_from_growth_rates(G, P0; rf=0.0, dt=1/252) → Vector{Float64}\n\nReconstruct a price path from excess growth rates and an initial price.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"simulation.html#Simulation","page":"Simulation","title":"Simulation","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"simulation.html#Overview","page":"Simulation","title":"Overview","text":"The simulate function generates synthetic paths from a fitted Jump-HMM.","category":"section"},{"location":"simulation.html#Algorithm","page":"Simulation","title":"Algorithm","text":"At each time step:\n\nWith probability (1 - ϵ): normal Markovian transition via row of T\nWith probability ϵ: a jump is triggered\nDuration K ~ Poisson(λ) consecutive steps\nEach jump step lands in bottom N_tail states (probability p_neg) or top N_tail states (probability 1 - p_neg), uniformly within the tail\nFor each state in the chain, an observation is sampled from the corresponding Student-t emission","category":"section"},{"location":"simulation.html#Usage","page":"Simulation","title":"Usage","text":"result = simulate(model, 252; n_paths=1000, seed=1234)\n\n# Access individual paths\npath = result.paths[1]\npath.states        # Vector{Int} — hidden state indices\npath.observations  # Vector{Float64} — sampled excess growth rates\npath.jumps         # Vector{Bool} — true at jump steps","category":"section"},{"location":"simulation.html#API","page":"Simulation","title":"API","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"simulation.html#Types","page":"Simulation","title":"Types","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"fitting.html#Fitting","page":"Fitting","title":"Fitting","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"fitting.html#Overview","page":"Fitting","title":"Overview","text":"The fit function constructs a JumpHiddenMarkovModel from a price time series. It performs the full pipeline:\n\nCompute excess growth rates from prices\nFit a Laplace distribution via MLE → build LaplacePartition with N states\nAssign observations to states\nEstimate the transition matrix by frequency counting\nFit per-state Student-t emissions (with fallback for sparse states)\nCompute the stationary distribution via T^50\nInitialize jump parameters with ϵ=0 (no jumps until tuned)","category":"section"},{"location":"fitting.html#Usage","page":"Fitting","title":"Usage","text":"model = fit(JumpHiddenMarkovModel, prices; rf=0.05, N=100, ν=5.0)","category":"section"},{"location":"fitting.html#API","page":"Fitting","title":"API","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"fitting.html#State-Partition","page":"Fitting","title":"State Partition","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"fitting.html#Transition-Matrix","page":"Fitting","title":"Transition Matrix","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"fitting.html#Emissions","page":"Fitting","title":"Emissions","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"fitting.html#Types","page":"Fitting","title":"Types","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"getting_started.html#Getting-Started","page":"Getting Started","title":"Getting Started","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"getting_started.html#Quick-Start:-Single-Asset","page":"Getting Started","title":"Quick Start: Single Asset","text":"using JumpHMM\n\n# Load your price data (Vector{Float64} of daily closing prices)\nprices = [...]\n\n# Step 1: Fit the base model (partition + transitions + emissions)\nmodel = fit(JumpHiddenMarkovModel, prices; rf=0.05, N=100, ν=5.0)\n\n# Step 2: Tune jump parameters via grid search\nmodel = tune(model, prices;\n    ϵ_range=range(1e-4, 2.5e-2, length=20),\n    λ_range=range(10, 160, length=16),\n    n_paths=200, w_κ=0.20)\n\n# Step 3: Simulate 1000 synthetic paths of 252 trading days\nresult = simulate(model, 252; n_paths=1000, seed=1234)\n\n# Step 4: Validate\nreport = validate(model, prices; n_paths=1000)\nprintln(\"KS pass rate: $(report.ks_pass_rate)\")\nprintln(\"Mean ACF-MAE: $(report.mean_acf_mae)\")","category":"section"},{"location":"getting_started.html#Quick-Start:-Multi-Asset-Portfolio","page":"Getting Started","title":"Quick Start: Multi-Asset Portfolio","text":"using JumpHMM\n\ntickers = [\"AAPL\", \"MSFT\", \"JPM\", \"XOM\", \"JNJ\"]\nprice_matrix = [...]  # (n_obs × 5) matrix\n\n# Fit marginals + Student-t copula (default)\nportfolio = fit(PortfolioModel, tickers, price_matrix;\n    dependence=StudentTCopula, rf=0.05, N=100)\n\n# Tune and simulate\nportfolio = tune(portfolio, price_matrix)\nport_result = simulate(portfolio, 252; n_paths=1000)\n\n# Validate each asset\nreports = validate(portfolio, price_matrix)\nfor (ticker, report) in reports\n    println(\"$ticker: KS=$(report.ks_pass_rate), ACF-MAE=$(report.mean_acf_mae)\")\nend","category":"section"},{"location":"getting_started.html#How-It-Works","page":"Getting Started","title":"How It Works","text":"The model is defined by five components:\n\nLaplace Partition: Continuous observations are discretized into N states (default 100) using equal-probability quantile bins of a fitted Laplace distribution\nTransition Matrix: Estimated by direct frequency counting of observed state-to-state transitions (no Baum-Welch/EM)\nEmissions: Per-state location-scale Student-t distributions with ν=5 degrees of freedom\nStationary Distribution: Computed via matrix exponentiation T^50\nJump Mechanism: With probability ϵ per step, the chain is forced into tail states for Poisson(λ) consecutive steps, producing volatility clustering\n\nThe fit → tune two-step workflow separates the deterministic model fitting (fast) from the stochastic jump parameter optimization (expensive grid search).","category":"section"},{"location":"validation.html#Validation","page":"Validation","title":"Validation","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"validation.html#Overview","page":"Validation","title":"Overview","text":"The validate function performs statistical validation by simulating paths and comparing them against empirical data using multiple metrics.","category":"section"},{"location":"validation.html#Metrics","page":"Validation","title":"Metrics","text":"For each simulated path, the following are computed:\n\nKS p-value: Kolmogorov-Smirnov two-sample test\nAD p-value: Anderson-Darling k-sample test\nWasserstein-1: Earth mover's distance\nHellinger: Histogram-based Hellinger distance\nACF-MAE: Mean absolute error of autocorrelation of |returns|\nExcess kurtosis: Simulated vs empirical","category":"section"},{"location":"validation.html#Usage","page":"Validation","title":"Usage","text":"report = validate(model, prices; n_paths=1000, α=0.05)\n\nreport.ks_pass_rate      # fraction of paths with KS p-value > α\nreport.ad_pass_rate      # fraction of paths with AD p-value > α\nreport.mean_acf_mae      # mean ACF-MAE across paths\nreport.mean_wasserstein   # mean Wasserstein-1 distance\nreport.mean_hellinger     # mean Hellinger distance\nreport.mean_kurtosis      # mean excess kurtosis across paths","category":"section"},{"location":"validation.html#API","page":"Validation","title":"API","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"validation.html#Types","page":"Validation","title":"Types","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"index.html#JumpHMM.jl","page":"Home","title":"JumpHMM.jl","text":"JumpHMM.jl is a Julia package implementing a Hybrid Hidden Markov Model with Poisson jump-diffusion for generating synthetic equity time series. The package implements the method described in:\n\nAlswaidan A. and Varner J. Hybrid Hidden Markov Model for Modeling Equity Excess Growth Rate Dynamics: A Discrete-State Approach with Jump-Diffusion. Cornell University.\n\nThe model reproduces three canonical stylized facts of financial returns:\n\nHeavy-tailed distributions (leptokurtosis)\nNegligible linear autocorrelation in raw returns\nPersistent volatility clustering (ARCH effect in absolute returns)","category":"section"},{"location":"index.html#Features","page":"Home","title":"Features","text":"Single-asset pipeline: fit → tune → simulate → validate → decode\nThree multi-asset dependence models: GaussianCopula, StudentTCopula, SingleIndexModel\nImmutable type system — all fitting/tuning operations return new instances\nSelf-contained — all finance helpers built in","category":"section"},{"location":"index.html#Installation","page":"Home","title":"Installation","text":"using Pkg\nPkg.add(url=\"https://github.com/varnerlab/JumpHMM.jl.git\")","category":"section"},{"location":"index.html#Disclaimer-and-Risks","page":"Home","title":"Disclaimer and Risks","text":"This content is offered solely for training and informational purposes. No offer or solicitation to buy or sell securities or derivative products or any investment or trading advice or strategy is made, given, or endorsed by the teaching team.\n\nTrading involves risk. Carefully review your financial situation before investing in securities, futures contracts, options, or commodity interests. Past performance, whether actual or indicated by historical tests of strategies, is no guarantee of future performance or success. Trading is generally inappropriate for someone with limited resources, investment or trading experience, or a low-risk tolerance. Only risk capital that is not required for living expenses.\n\nYou are fully responsible for any investment or trading decisions you make. Such decisions should be based solely on evaluating your financial circumstances, investment or trading objectives, risk tolerance, and liquidity needs.","category":"section"}]
